 "_note": "THE INPUT MASK ENGINE (U08 · textfield `mask=`). Token grammar: '#' a digit, 'A' an ASCII letter, '*' an ASCII letter or digit, '\\\\x' the literal x, anything else a literal. THE LAZY-LITERAL FORMAT LAW: walking the mask, a literal is emitted only while unplaced raw characters remain — so '(415' never shows a dangling ') '. THE EXTRACTION LAW: a character is SIGNIFICANT iff at least one placeholder class appearing in the mask accepts it; `bind` receives the significant characters (the UNMASKED value) and `boundFormatted` the display. THE PLACEMENT LAW: maskEdit's `raw` is the significant characters the mask ACCEPTED \u2014 a character no remaining slot will take is dropped from the value as well as from the display, so `bind` never carries text the field does not show and a mixed-class mask ('AA-####' fed '12ab34') cannot fill to capacity with the characters it refused. THE EDIT LAW: maskEdit(mask, prev, selStart, selEnd, insert) replaces prev[selStart:selEnd] with insert — the one primitive UITextField shouldChangeCharactersIn, the web beforeinput target range and Compose's TextFieldValue diff all reduce to. THE SEPARATOR SWALLOW LAW: a deletion whose removed span holds NO significant character extends to swallow the nearest significant character to its LEFT, else the nearest to its RIGHT — this is why backspacing over ') ' deletes a digit instead of doing nothing. THE CARET LAW: the new caret sits immediately after the Nth significant character of the new display, where N is the number of significant characters that preceded the edit point (head + insert), clamped to capacity. Every expected value here was computed by an independent scratch implementation, never by a kernel under test.",
